00:40:48,892 --> 00:40:50,653
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you get it scuffed?

809
00:40:51,427 --> 00:40:55,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, a lot of times it would hit the ground or something, you know, and put a scuff on it.

810
00:40:56,009 --> 00:40:59,371
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of times now, if it hits the ground, how far do I want to see the ball?

811
00:41:00,491 --> 00:41:02,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they go through a lot of them now.

812
00:41:04,153 --> 00:41:15,238
[SPEAKER_00]: But I guess the original Emory ball had a... Well, it used to have a spot on the belt, a little piece of Emory paper, and they could just... Well, that's before my time, too.

813
00:41:16,939 --> 00:41:17,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you know it?

814
00:41:17,539 --> 00:41:19,620
[SPEAKER_00]: That red paper, it wasn't a great...

815
00:41:22,103 --> 00:41:24,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarence Mitchell, he could left-hand a spitter.

816
00:41:25,145 --> 00:41:26,446
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd really slobber off.

817
00:41:27,467 --> 00:41:36,173
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd hit him down a pepper martin, you know, hit the ground ball down a pepper, get ahold of that ball, and probably get ahold of that spit, and the way, the damn thing would go way up into the bleachers.

818
00:41:39,815 --> 00:41:49,762
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought the infielders got good dirt in their hands before that ball was thrown, so they could... Oh, I used to spit in my hat and rub it like that, but I... That wasn't frowned upon?

