00:52:41,797 --> 00:52:46,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Then my first game in the Major League, of course, with Detroit.

1045
00:52:48,421 --> 00:52:52,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Then that no-hit game I pitched to the Buffaloes when I was with Syracuse.

1046
00:52:54,105 --> 00:52:56,887
[SPEAKER_00]: I can still see that last out, the big pop-up to third base.

1047
00:52:56,927 --> 00:53:00,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's so many different things.

1048
00:53:01,228 --> 00:53:03,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you walk many, or hit any, that game?

1049
00:53:03,368 --> 00:53:04,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't know.

1050
00:53:04,689 --> 00:53:05,169
[SPEAKER_00]: I never did.

1051
00:53:05,289 --> 00:53:06,409
[SPEAKER_00]: I always had good control.

1052
00:53:06,429 --> 00:53:08,310
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't a perfect game, though, was it?

1053
00:53:08,350 --> 00:53:08,570
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1054
00:53:08,750 --> 00:53:09,450
[SPEAKER_00]: No hit at all?

1055
00:53:10,970 --> 00:53:12,190
[SPEAKER_00]: That must have been a big thrill.

1056
00:53:12,530 --> 00:53:18,152
[SPEAKER_00]: I probably walked so many, but I know there was a line driver, so I was in the second baseman.

1057
00:53:18,192 --> 00:53:19,512
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a double play on it.

1058
00:53:19,812 --> 00:53:20,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I can remember that.

1059
00:53:22,313 --> 00:53:25,453
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't always remember that high pop-up.

1060
00:53:25,593 --> 00:53:27,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1061
00:53:28,154 --> 00:53:28,714
[SPEAKER_01]: What was the?

1062
00:53:30,057 --> 00:53:32,398
[SPEAKER_01]: lowest hit game you pitched in the majors you ever had?

1063
00:53:32,538 --> 00:53:33,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, gosh, I don't know.

1064
00:53:33,958 --> 00:53:37,139
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a couple of one-hit games, a couple of two-hit games.

1065
00:53:38,580 --> 00:53:41,441
[SPEAKER_00]: But like I say, I never started too much.

1066
00:53:41,781 --> 00:53:43,461
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done an awful lot of remit for it.

1067
00:53:45,602 --> 00:53:49,423
[SPEAKER_00]: I grew up with a base that's full.

1068
00:53:49,483 --> 00:53:53,925
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember one day in St. Louis, playing against Boston, that's when Hornsby was there.

1069
00:53:53,985 --> 00:53:56,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Les Bell.

1070
00:53:57,026 --> 00:53:58,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Hornsby and Les Bell.

1071
00:53:59,442 --> 00:54:05,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Chardelle was pitching for us, and they had that tuberculosis day in St. Louis at that time.

1072
00:54:06,104 --> 00:54:18,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And we were ahead one run going into the ninth inning, and Chardelle pitching, and Boston got the bases full, and Hornsby was the hitter.

1073
00:54:19,807 --> 00:54:20,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So who goes in but me?

1074
00:54:22,948 --> 00:54:24,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Put me in the ballgame.

1075
00:54:25,688 --> 00:54:28,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I strike Hornsby out, and made one out.

1076
00:54:29,345 --> 00:54:31,127
[SPEAKER_00]: I struck Bell out and made two out.

1077
00:54:32,268 --> 00:54:36,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I forget the next hitter, but anyway, it was a big pop-up to the third baseman.

1078
00:54:37,813 --> 00:54:40,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So I got Hornsby, Bell, and that pop-up.

1079
00:54:41,015 --> 00:54:43,698
[SPEAKER_00]: We win the ball game.

1080
00:54:43,958 --> 00:54:47,061
[SPEAKER_00]: That, to me, was one of the greatest jobs I ever made.

1081
00:54:47,841 --> 00:54:49,122
[SPEAKER_00]: That's real pressure pitching.

1082
00:54:50,043 --> 00:54:54,747
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to pitch right in there to Roger, and I pitched right in there to Les Bell.

1083
00:54:59,890 --> 00:55:03,173
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like high fast balls, that crowd of them.

1084
00:55:03,213 --> 00:55:13,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Hartman, and Demery, and you know those fellas that when Demery first came up, he was a high ball, he was supposed to be a high ball hitter, but I'd pitch high at home.

1085
00:55:14,402 --> 00:55:17,125
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd pitch my own strength, but there's not much to say over here.

1086
00:55:17,145 --> 00:55:17,785
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to hit it.

1087
00:55:18,125 --> 00:55:21,969
[SPEAKER_00]: I got the ball, you've got to hit it.

1088
00:55:21,989 --> 00:55:24,291
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't say that as long as you make a hit, you've got a chance.

1089
00:55:24,651 --> 00:55:25,932
[SPEAKER_00]: You walk up, you don't have a chance.

1090
00:55:27,261 --> 00:55:31,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, there's times that base opens, and you will walk it.

1091
00:55:31,422 --> 00:55:36,463
[SPEAKER_01]: How about your move to first base?

1092
00:55:36,483 --> 00:55:38,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any problems with that at all?

1093
00:55:38,544 --> 00:55:39,204
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1094
00:55:39,244 --> 00:55:39,564
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1095
00:55:43,625 --> 00:55:43,785
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1096
00:55:43,825 --> 00:55:47,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that catches pretty good arms, too, you know what I mean?

1097
00:55:48,006 --> 00:55:53,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I'd throw over to first base,

1098
00:55:54,284 --> 00:56:01,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Like I said, as a fastball pitcher, they don't get little breaks a lot of times on the fastball pitchers.

1099
00:56:01,228 --> 00:56:05,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially the high ballers right there with, you know, Phil Goodow would have been.

1100
00:56:10,193 --> 00:56:13,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you really met and played with some great people, didn't you?

1101
00:56:13,956 --> 00:56:14,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

1102
00:56:16,417 --> 00:56:16,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Yep.

1103
00:56:16,777 --> 00:56:22,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any, uh,

1104
00:56:23,603 --> 00:56:27,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you recall any very funny incidents happening on a ball field?

1105
00:56:27,624 --> 00:56:28,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Do what?

1106
00:56:28,565 --> 00:56:32,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you recall any funny, humorous things happening in a game?

1107
00:56:32,886 --> 00:56:33,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think the funniest...

1108
00:56:35,167 --> 00:56:39,869
[SPEAKER_00]: I probably should have said, I'd say this, was with Flint Rembrandt.

1109
00:56:39,889 --> 00:56:42,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Flint Rembrandt was, you know, he used to drink quite a bit, too.

1110
00:56:44,030 --> 00:56:46,731
[SPEAKER_00]: And he came out one time in Cincinnati to start the ball game.

1111
00:56:46,751 --> 00:56:50,693
[SPEAKER_00]: He got in that pitcher's mouth and he couldn't get set.

1112
00:56:53,277 --> 00:56:58,118
[SPEAKER_00]: He threw the first one and it went over the catcher's head and got darned net, you know.

1113
00:56:58,138 --> 00:57:01,880
[SPEAKER_00]: He threw another one just about as bad, and then he walks off the mound.

1114
00:57:01,900 --> 00:57:05,681
[SPEAKER_00]: He goes over to the groundkeeper's place over there and he gets a shovel.

1115
00:57:05,701 --> 00:57:11,682
[SPEAKER_00]: He comes out with the rubber, you know, and starts digging a hole in that shovel where he can get his foot.

1116
00:57:14,283 --> 00:57:18,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that didn't help anything, so they finally knew what the hell was the matter with him.

1117
00:57:18,824 --> 00:57:21,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I thank God about it, but to me that was the funniest way to go.

1118
00:57:22,692 --> 00:57:24,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Why'd he come out with that shovel?

1119
00:57:25,053 --> 00:57:27,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, if he got his feet lower down, the ball wouldn't go so high up.

1120
00:57:28,015 --> 00:57:32,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he'd get his toe over that.

1121
00:57:32,357 --> 00:57:38,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any pictures, extra pictures of yourself you might be able to autograph for me?

1122
00:57:38,539 --> 00:57:39,600
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something I don't have.

1123
00:57:39,640 --> 00:57:40,480
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish I could get some.

1124
00:57:40,500 --> 00:57:41,241
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have any?

1125
00:57:42,061 --> 00:57:42,801
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know who to.

1126
00:57:42,842 --> 00:57:44,222
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish I did have them.

1127
00:57:44,262 --> 00:57:45,723
[SPEAKER_00]: I really don't have a picture.

1128
00:57:46,023 --> 00:57:47,003
[SPEAKER_01]: You sent them all?

1129
00:57:47,124 --> 00:57:48,044
[SPEAKER_00]: I get letters.

1130
00:57:48,484 --> 00:57:50,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I get two or three or four letters every week.

1131
00:57:56,615 --> 00:58:00,659
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish I knew where I could get some of those pictures.

1132
00:58:00,719 --> 00:58:05,483
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd like to send them to the kids, but I really don't have one.

1133
00:58:05,523 --> 00:58:10,688
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure as heck give it to you.

1134
00:58:11,068 --> 00:58:13,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You did have them, but they've all gone, is that it?

1135
00:58:13,350 --> 00:58:13,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

1136
00:58:19,848 --> 00:58:28,353
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to have so many of them as chewing gum and stuff like that, you know, to sign up for.

1137
00:58:28,413 --> 00:58:30,274
[SPEAKER_01]: I may have one of you on a chewing gum.

1138
00:58:30,294 --> 00:58:33,536
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you probably have.

1139
00:58:33,556 --> 00:58:35,898
[SPEAKER_00]: So often you get a regular postcard size, you know.

1140
00:58:35,918 --> 00:58:39,160
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be either Phillies or mostly Philadelphia.

1141
00:58:41,001 --> 00:58:45,704
[SPEAKER_00]: And then a lot of them were the Cardinals, and not too many were Detroit.

1142
00:58:50,550 --> 00:58:52,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's been a very interesting interview.

1143
00:58:54,152 --> 00:58:55,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, thank you very much for it.

1144
00:58:55,932 --> 00:59:07,238
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish there was a lot more, but like I say, a fellow could keep asking questions and probably always write a book if he had time.

1145
00:59:07,958 --> 00:59:13,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Looks like 26 years here from your first year with Portland down to Vancouver.

1146
00:59:13,380 --> 00:59:14,321
[SPEAKER_01]: 27 years.

1147
00:59:14,561 --> 00:59:18,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Last year, when I went up to Vancouver to manage.

1148
00:59:20,311 --> 00:59:22,212
[SPEAKER_00]: That was in, what year was that?

1149
00:59:22,272 --> 00:59:22,572
[SPEAKER_00]: 46.

1150
00:59:23,112 --> 00:59:23,292
[SPEAKER_00]: 46.

1151
00:59:24,872 --> 00:59:25,753
[SPEAKER_00]: And I quit in June.

1152
00:59:25,813 --> 00:59:27,813
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't like it.

1153
00:59:27,893 --> 00:59:28,554
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't like it.

1154
00:59:28,634 --> 00:59:30,594
[SPEAKER_00]: I went scouting with the Yankees.

1155
00:59:31,094 --> 00:59:32,355
[SPEAKER_01]: How long did you scout with the Yankees?

1156
00:59:32,415 --> 00:59:35,676
[SPEAKER_00]: About three years, a little better, with Joe Devine.

1157
00:59:35,856 --> 00:59:37,416
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was up in the Northwest here.

1158
00:59:39,817 --> 00:59:40,718
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I had.

1159
00:59:41,698 --> 00:59:45,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I'd be working at Reynolds Aluminum Plant, too, at that time.

1160
00:59:46,019 --> 00:59:48,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I had a very, very farm out here.

1161
00:59:49,609 --> 00:59:56,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'd catch the colleges and the high schools, you know, around town here and send in reports.

1162
00:59:57,956 --> 01:00:00,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody you recommend ever make it?

1163
01:00:00,578 --> 01:00:01,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, a couple of them.

1164
01:00:01,558 --> 01:00:03,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Morrison was one of them.

1165
01:00:03,400 --> 01:00:11,927
[SPEAKER_00]: And, oh, I had a couple of good-looking kids, but then everybody was after them, and they turned out to be just couldn't make it.

1166
01:00:14,208 --> 01:00:16,130
[SPEAKER_00]: At the most, bonus every year was $4,000.

1167
01:00:16,190 --> 01:00:18,211
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, it was a high bonus, $4,000.

1168
01:00:19,630 --> 01:00:21,350
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't get nothing when I went up.

1169
01:00:21,430 --> 01:00:23,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I got $400 a month for the tight job.

1170
01:00:23,951 --> 01:00:25,171
[SPEAKER_00]: With Detroit?

1171
01:00:26,391 --> 01:00:27,312
[SPEAKER_01]: How about later on?

1172
01:00:27,352 --> 01:00:28,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you get much more?

1173
01:00:28,972 --> 01:00:30,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, about $5,000.

1174
01:00:30,372 --> 01:00:31,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And about Thompson?

1175
01:00:31,953 --> 01:00:33,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Thompson.

1176
01:00:33,373 --> 01:00:35,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Ruth, he was the highest price paid.

1177
01:00:35,473 --> 01:00:37,914
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was supposed to be $80,000.

1178
01:00:38,054 --> 01:00:40,114
[SPEAKER_01]: In the middle of the Depression, too.

1179
01:00:40,754 --> 01:00:42,495
[SPEAKER_00]: What would he get now?

1180
01:00:42,635 --> 01:00:47,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unbelievable how high he could hit them.

1181
01:00:49,235 --> 01:00:54,198
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen him hit many a ball up in the infield of Whitbow a little bit, and it just staggered, you know.

1182
01:00:55,839 --> 01:00:57,520
[SPEAKER_00]: He put the second plate standing up.

1183
01:01:00,982 --> 01:01:04,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was quite a player.

1184
01:01:04,964 --> 01:01:08,346
[SPEAKER_01]: The very first game I ever saw, he had a home run.

1185
01:01:08,446 --> 01:01:09,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, where was this at?

1186
01:01:09,607 --> 01:01:10,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Cleveland in 1929.

1187
01:01:10,587 --> 01:01:11,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

1188
01:01:12,468 --> 01:01:13,409
[SPEAKER_00]: I grew up in Cleveland.

1189
01:01:14,289 --> 01:01:16,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that right field wasn't too awful far away, either.

1190
01:01:18,293 --> 01:01:19,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Centerfield was the clubhouse.

1191
01:01:20,815 --> 01:01:24,877
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember one time Spoke, we all called him Spoke, the speaker.

1192
01:01:24,897 --> 01:01:38,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I'd pitch him outside and he'd hit it right down that left field line just before it got down to, used to be a, left the stand, used to switch around a little bit, but damn boy, it went right in between the gate like this.

1193
01:01:38,785 --> 01:01:39,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, is that right?

1194
01:01:41,447 --> 01:01:41,987
[SPEAKER_00]: What was it?

1195
01:01:42,007 --> 01:01:43,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it was a two-base hit.

1196
01:01:43,188 --> 01:01:47,310
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what that was.

1197
01:01:47,711 --> 01:01:49,352
[SPEAKER_01]: People like Averill were my heroes.

1198
01:01:49,372 --> 01:01:51,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, he was a little later with Cleveland.

1199
01:01:52,214 --> 01:01:54,355
[SPEAKER_00]: They had Luke and Joey Sowell.

1200
01:01:56,296 --> 01:02:00,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Luke Sowell was catching, and oh, gosh, I forget those.

1201
01:02:00,198 --> 01:02:03,340
[SPEAKER_01]: They had Eddie Morgan.

1202
01:02:03,780 --> 01:02:04,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1203
01:02:05,121 --> 01:02:08,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Like I say, some clubs I can remember, like Detroit, some I can remember.

1204
01:02:09,203 --> 01:02:09,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Every player.

1205
01:02:11,384 --> 01:02:13,445
[SPEAKER_00]: And Philadelphia, most of them.

1206
01:02:13,646 --> 01:02:16,127
[SPEAKER_00]: And Cincinnati, because I was only in Cincinnati a month.

1207
01:02:17,195 --> 01:02:26,377
[SPEAKER_00]: When Jimmy Wilson, like I said, when Jimmy Wilson come through with the Phillies, he said, I'm going to take you back to Philadelphia with me.

1208
01:02:26,497 --> 01:02:28,038
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, oh, get out of here.

1209
01:02:29,758 --> 01:02:31,318
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a bad box, you know.

1210
01:02:32,919 --> 01:02:34,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure enough, the service was over.

1211
01:02:34,819 --> 01:02:39,500
[SPEAKER_00]: I got called from Cincinnati, you're traded to Philadelphia.

1212
01:02:39,520 --> 01:02:43,821
[SPEAKER_00]: That was when Shulman Riggins' client hand says, Ruble, come over to

1213
01:02:48,544 --> 01:02:54,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Cincinnati from Philadelphia and it wasn't it wasn't

